## 5. Precedents and concrete lessons

Two independently useful implementation precedents (different codebases, different lessons), plus component precedents:

### 5.1 Audacity (whole-product precedent — project format, recovery, nondestructive storage)
Documented behavior [S16]: single `.aup3` file; only Audacity can open it; undo history is *not* saved with the project; automatic crash recovery on relaunch; compaction of temporary space on close; audio referenced from the original until an edit is applied, after which both old and new audio are retained (undoable edits double the footprint of edited regions); nondestructive trim *hides* audio, causing "project bloat" with no permanent-delete command; WAL/SHM temp files; FAT/FAT32 destinations blocked for the 4 GB reason.
**Lessons taken:** (a) keep exchange artifacts reviewable and mergeable — hence the folder bundle (§3.2); (b) recovery must be automatic and explicit — hence the recovery journal (§8); (c) nondestructive editing must not silently accumulate hidden bulk — hence per-source caches rather than per-edit audio blocks, and a size report in the bundle (§7.2); (d) communicate clearly what is and is not saved (undo depth) instead of implying otherwise.

### 5.2 BBC audiowaveform (component precedent — derived waveform caches)
Documented behavior [S03, S01]: waveform data = min/max over groups of N input samples (zoom, default 256), channels combined to mono unless `--split-channels`; binary `.dat`/JSON outputs; "it is not possible to set a zoom level less than that used to create the original waveform data file"; Google Test suite; GPL-3.0 [S01]; active development moved to Codeberg [S03] (L05).
**Lessons taken:** (a) the overview is a *pure derived artifact* — our cache keys are content hashes plus generation parameters (§7.2); (b) generate at the finest needed level and derive coarser views by aggregation, because you cannot zoom below the generation level [S03]; (c) a cache file is untrusted input — issue #159 (§7 of this document) proves that even a first-party cache format needs fatal, field-level validation on load.

### 5.3 Component precedents
- **libsamplerate streaming contract** [S14]: `src_process` may consume fewer frames than given (`input_frames_used`) and produce fewer than requested (`output_frames_gen`); ratio changes are *smoothed* unless `src_set_ratio` forces a step; `src_reset` is required between unrelated pieces. A naive one-shot conversion loop truncates audio — the chunked renderer (§7.4) is designed directly against this contract.
- **libsndfile I/O semantics** [S12]: seeks are in multichannel frames and return −1 outside the file; reads past EOF return short/zero without error; the chunk API retrieves arbitrary metadata fail-safely; float/int scaling has documented traps (Notes 1–2).
- **SQLite atomic commit discipline** [S17]: commit happens at rollback-journal deletion; two fsync phases; recovery hinges on the journal's existence. Adopted as the pattern for manifest saves and interrupted-save recovery (§8.1) regardless of storage engine.

**Protected-discovery note (V14):** discovery deliberately spanned mechanism families outside whole-product competitors — waveform caching (audiowaveform), transcript-cue sync (OHMS, L01 — unreachable in this environment, recorded not claimed), standardized cue interchange (WebVTT [S18]), storage atomicity (SQLite [S17]), resampling/I/O (libsamplerate/libsndfile [S12, S14]) — alongside the Audacity whole-product lane. Breadth was cut off where captures failed (OHMS) or pages truncated (WebVTT deep sections); those are recorded as leads, not silently dropped.

## 6. Timeline contract: positions, cues, rounding, boundaries

### 6.1 Domains
- **Source sample position** `s` — integer, native to a specific source file at its own rate.
- **Clip-local position** — integer offset from clip head within the selected source range.
- **Project time** `p` — integer frames at the project rate (default 48 kHz, CHOICE).
- **Displayed timecode** — derived, never stored as truth: `ms = (p*1000 + rate//2) // rate` (round-half-up in integer math), formatted `HH:MM:SS.mmm`; a samples readout is available. EXECUTED W2 pins this rule's behavior (sub-ms truncation, exact half-ms round-up, negative clamp) and demonstrates why float-seconds are forbidden: `f"{2.675:.2f}"` yields `"2.67"`.
- **Exported cue positions** — derived in the export domain by the same floor rule from `p`, and always accompanied by source identity (§6.5).

### 6.2 Mapping (all-integer, floor)
For a clip with source range `[t, t+len)` (source samples, half-open) placed at project position `q`:

```
p(s) = q + floor((s - t) * Rproj / Rsrc)      for s in [t, t+len)   (else: orphan)
s(p) = t + floor((p - q) * Rsrc / Rproj)      for p in [q, q+Lproj) (half-open)
Lproj = len * Rproj // Rsrc
```

EXECUTED W1: at 1:1 rates this is exactly involutive and boundary-exact (trim-start sample → clip head; final sample → last project frame; one-past-end → orphan). At 44.1 kHz → 48 kHz the two floor maps are **not** inverses of each other: 4,516 of 4,547 probed samples fail exact round-trip. Therefore:

- **Cues and regions anchor in source samples** (authoritative). Project positions are *derived*, recomputed after every edit, and never the sole stored truth. A rate conversion, trim, inserted gap, or transition can change derived positions — never identity (brief requirement).

### 6.3 Rounding and boundary rules
- All edits (trims, splits, selection edges) snap to **source samples** at command creation; the UI may draw in milliseconds, but the stored command is integral. One rounding per command, at creation.
- Clip domains are half-open `[start, end)`. A cue anchored exactly at `t` maps to the clip head; a cue anchored at `t+len` (the exclusive end) is a **boundary cue**: it maps to the clip tail position, is flagged `boundary`, and — at a butt join — belongs to the earlier clip. It never silently slides onto different content.
- **Trimmed-away anchors become orphaned, visibly:** the cue keeps its original source anchor, its last valid mapping, the clip id, and a revision-chain id of the edit that orphaned it; the transcript pane and cue table show an explicit "orphaned" state with a jump-to-nearest-surviving-content affordance. No automatic re-anchoring to different content, ever (brief requirement).
- **Inserted gaps** are project-domain objects (project start + length at project rate) containing no source. Cues cannot anchor inside a gap; an attempt snaps to the nearest clip edge with a recorded warning.
- **Transitions** (§6.4) overlap two clips; a derived position inside a crossfade is exported with the flag `in_transition` and an accuracy note (±crossfade length). Source anchors remain exact.
- **Displayed duration honesty:** a computed duration is treated as a claim about numbers, not about assembled content — render verification (§10, golden-file checks) is the evidence that content was assembled correctly (brief requirement).

### 6.4 Joins and transitions
A join is either **butt** (clip A's last frame followed by clip B's first) or a **crossfade** of `D` project frames (default 5 ms = 240 frames at 48 kHz; range 0–100 ms; equal-power ramps; CHOICE). Crossfade parameters live only in the *render recipe* / edit command (they are rendered operations), while preview uses the same computation so what you audit is what exports. Channel mapping during joins follows §6.6; mono sources follow the project channel plan.

### 6.5 Exported cue table (per cue)
`cue_id; label; project timecode; project sample; export-domain position; source_id; source filename; source sha256; source rate; source sample anchor; clip_id; state (anchored | boundary | orphaned | in_transition); provenance (editor, timestamp, originating edit revision)`. TSV/JSON export this verbatim; the WebVTT opportunity export (§9.1) carries the identity fields in a comment line per cue. The next editor can therefore always answer "which audio does this cue point at?" without trusting our timecode math.

### 6.6 Channel mapping
Each clip carries an explicit table: source channel index → project bus (left/right/mono-center/custom-named), or *unassigned*. Unassigning a channel is an explicit command in the undo history; nothing is dropped by default. Voice (mono) typically maps to both buses; a stereo backup maps L/R; the noise bed follows the plan of its track. If an export recipe requests fewer channels than the plan needs, export **fails loudly** — no silent downmix (brief requirement on honest channel handling).

## 7. Architecture and resources

### 7.1 Components
- **EditModel** — pure data + command objects (undo/redo by inverse commands; bounded depth 200, CHOICE). No audio code, no Qt types — fully unit-testable.
- **MediaAccess** — per-source readers (libsndfile [S12]); windowed reads (`sf_seek` + `sf_readf_float`, 1–2 s windows ≈ 750 KiB for stereo float — W3); single internal float32 domain (§2).
- **WaveformCache** — derived pyramids per source (§7.2).
- **RenderService** — cancellable chunked jobs: overview build, export mix (§7.4).
- **ProjectStore** — manifest load/validate/save (§8), bundle and recovery management.
- **JobManager** — one worker thread per heavy job, cooperative cancel flags, progress objects bound to UI; heavy jobs never run on the UI thread.
- **UI shell** — Qt widgets; timeline, transcript pane, provenance inspector, keyboard map (§7.6).

**CHOICE (bounded alternatives):** the audio core is exactly the two pinned C libraries; the language/UI are separable. If Python performance proves insufficient for scrub on the target hardware, the bounded alternative is a C++/Qt shell over the *same* pinned libraries, keeping every data contract identical. A GStreamer-based I/O layer is the second alternative if exotic inputs are ever demanded (L07) — not selected because seek determinism and metadata capture are easier to audit against libsndfile's documented semantics [S12].

### 7.2 Waveform cache identity and invalidation
- Cache key: `wfp1 | sha256(source bytes) | channel_view | samples_per_pixel | generator_version`.
- One pyramid per source per channel view, generated at the finest overview level needed (default 256 samples/point → for 2 h at 48 kHz mono ≈ 6.7 M frames → ≈ 6.75 M/256 ≈ 26.4 k min/max points ≈ ~106 KB at int16 pairs — trivially small; coarser views are aggregated in memory for draw). Generation-level rule adopted from audiowaveform [S03].
- Invalidation is identity-based: any change of source hash invalidates every cache for that source; **edits never invalidate caches** because caches are per-source, not per-timeline. Stale-orphaned caches are garbage-collected by manifest audit.
- A cache file is **untrusted input**: on load, every header field is validated (magic/version, spp ≥ 1, declared point count ≤ ceil(frames/spp)+1, file length matches) and a violation is a *fatal* cache miss → rebuild. This rule exists because of the pinned failure below.

### 7.3 Issue/fix/regression chain (required investigation)

**Issue:** bbc/audiowaveform #159, "Crash when parsing malformed DAT and FLAC files" (opened 2022-01-16 by an external reporter doing fuzz testing) [S04]. A 22-byte crafted `.dat` declared ~4.18 billion points and **samples-per-pixel 0**; the rescaler divided by that zero → `SIGFPE` in `WaveformRescaler::rescale` (reporter's gdb session; classified PROBABLY_NOT_EXPLOITABLE). The same report included a malformed-FLAC crash whose stack lies inside libFLAC/libsndfile, not audiowaveform's code.

**Fix:** commit `63c8bd4baadc19e6e19c60dd3e0894f8e551215e`, "Improved .dat file validation / See #159" (2022-01-16, signed) [S05]: single file `src/WaveformBuffer.cpp` (+24/−21); the `sample_rate ≥ 1` and `samples_per_pixel ≥ 2` checks were **moved into header parsing and made fatal** (`return false`), where previously they ran only after the whole buffer was read and merely set `success = false` while the caller still used the invalid buffer. That is the entire causal mechanism: validation existed but did not stop execution.

**Release:** v1.6.0 — release commit `49e36682f1bcebaa23717db3f0b6074ee15810f6` (2022-02-18) [S06]; CHANGELOG v1.6.0 "Fixed crash when reading malformed .dat files"; issue closed "completed" the same day [S02, S04]. **Applicability:** v1.6.0 (2022-02-18) and later; earlier releases crash on such input.

**Regression test:** `test/WaveformBufferTest.cpp` at master contains `shouldNotLoadDataFileWithSampleRateBelowMinimum` and `shouldNotLoadDataFileWithSamplesPerPixelBelowMinimum`, asserting `load()` returns **false** with exact error strings, against fixtures `test/data/sample_rate_too_low.dat` / `samples_per_pixel_too_low.dat` [S07, S08].

**Causal limits (preserved honestly):** the fixtures date from the 2013 initial import commit `f69dfc0` [S09], and the commit search for "#159" finds only the source-only fix commit [S10] — so **no new test was added with the fix**; the pre-existing tests cover the fatal-failure behavior, but I could not pin a test that specifically reproduces the rescaler SIGFPE. The malformed-FLAC half of the issue was **not** addressed by this commit (crash is in libFLAC/libsndfile code per the reporter's stack) yet the issue was closed "completed" — that ambiguity is recorded, not resolved.

**Lessons applied:** (a) derived files (waveform caches, manifests, exports) are validated field-by-field with fatal failure and rebuild (§7.2, §8); (b) "checks exist" ≠ "checks stop execution" — code review and tests must assert the *fatal* path, exactly the delta this fix made; (c) fix provenance is verified by release + changelog + issue closure together, not by a patch link alone.

### 7.4 Rendering and resampling (bounded, cancellable)
Export iterates the timeline in ~4 s windows: compute each clip's overlapping source range in integers → `sf_seek`/read float window → feed libsamplerate with the **streaming contract**: loop `src_process` until `input_frames_used` consumes the window, set `end_of_input` only on the final window, `src_reset` at every clip start (never across unrelated material), step ratio via `src_set_ratio` (no smoothing across clip boundaries) [S14]. Crossfades applied in float32 before quantization. Export gain applied in the recipe; preview gain never enters this path (§1). Clip census: samples at float ±1.0 boundary counted; final 24-bit quantization applies clipping and the export record reports peak + clipped-sample count (honest clipping treatment; no automatic limiting in v1). Default export resampler quality: `SRC_SINC_MEDIUM_QUALITY` (CHOICE; exact per-quality behavior is the library's documented domain, not re-claimed here).

### 7.5 Export lifecycle and the cancelled-render rule
Output is written to `exports/<name>.tmp`; only after full write + fsync + SHA-256 does the finalizer rename it to the final name and write the export record `status=complete, checksum, parameters, generator version`; the cue table file is written **after** audio finalization. **Cancel at any point → the tmp file is deleted (or renamed `.cancelled`), an export record `status=cancelled` is written, and no complete-export label or manifest entry ever appears** (brief requirement). Reopen scans `exports/` for `.tmp`/`.cancelled`/dangling records and shows a recovery list; a record whose checksum fails verification is displayed as *failed verification*, never as complete.

### 7.6 8 GB laptop budget, seeking, progress/cancel
- Memory: whole-file float access of a 2 h stereo program is 2.57 GiB (W3 EXECUTED) — forbidden by design; all access is windowed (750 KiB per 2 s stereo window, W3). Working-set target ≤ 600 MB RSS with transcript + two zoom levels resident (CHOICE — a **target to investigate**, not a measured claim; §10 includes the soak test that would establish it).
- Seeking: sample-exact via `sf_seek` to the source anchor [S12]. For lossy sources (MP3/Opus/Vorbis) codec delay/padding at seek boundaries is **not verified** at the pinned version (lead L11; audiowaveform's own history shows MP3 info-frame offset is a real defect class [S02]); until the L11 round-trip test passes, lossy sources carry a provenance flag and the scrub cursor reports decoded positions.
- Progress + cancel exist for both heavy operations (overview build, export), with phase labels (hashing / indexing / writing) and a hard rule: a cancelled job's artifacts never masquerade as complete (§7.5).

### 7.7 Editor-facing requirements (v1 acceptance features)
Keyboard-first transport and navigation (play/pause, scrub, next/prev cue/region, zoom, split-at-cursor, join, undo/redo); every command reachable without mouse; transcript pane with adjustable type size, high-contrast theme, and per-cue state coloring (anchored/orphaned/boundary); solo/mute and channel-choice per track; a persistent "current edit" indicator (last command + affected clip, with jump-to-undo history); provenance inspector per cue/region/source (hash, rates, chunk captures, ambiguity flags, edit chain); errors surfaced inline + in a copyable log; undo/redo with depth display. These are CHOICES of emphasis under the brief's obligations; automatic transcription, noise reduction, shared live editing, and cloud accounts are optional opportunities (§9) and are not requirements.

## 8. Recovery, interruption, and resource limits

### 8.1 Save protocol
Manifest saves follow the journal-then-rename discipline modeled on SQLite's documented atomic commit (commit point = removal of the journal; two flush phases; recovery keys off the journal's existence) [S17]: write `manifest.json.new` → fsync file → fsync directory → `os.replace` → previous manifest rotated to `manifest.bak` (2 generations). A crash mid-save leaves `manifest.json` untouched and `manifest.json.new` discarded at next open. UNEXECUTED as code in this stage; the kill-during-save test in §10 is what would evidence it.

### 8.2 Interrupted operations
- **Import:** hashing + indexing writes one sidecar; interrupted import leaves the source unregistered and the sidecar marked incomplete; retry is idempotent (hash-keyed).
- **Overview build:** same tmp-then-rename as exports; interrupted/invalid caches are cache-misses (§7.2).
- **Save/close:** §8.1; the reopen scan also reports leftover `*.new`/tmp files.
- **Crash recovery:** on open, `recovery/` journals (unapplied command batches) are listed and offered individually — replay or discard — mirroring the "automatic, explicit" recovery lesson from Audacity [S16].
- **Changed/missing media:** §3.3; no overwrite of originals anywhere.

### 8.3 Support limits (stated, not promised)
Two-hour, ~10-source projects are the design target for v1 (brief); the target class is investigated by the §10 soak/perf tests, and this proposal claims **no performance number for any component**. Formats/channels per §2. No network access is required by v1; OHMS/web integrations would be separate optional work (L01).

## 9. Opportunities and alternatives

### 9.1 Opportunities (useful, optional)
1. **Reviewed-region tracking (headline opportunity):** regions already carry named spans + provenance; adding review state (who heard it, when, verdict) gives the team the "next editor understands what was heard" property almost for free — it is editorial bookkeeping, not DSP.
2. **WebVTT cue export** [S18]: standardized time-aligned text interchange (captions/chapters/metadata) for committees and web players; identity fields ride along as comments. Deep syntax sections unpinned (L02) — implement only after pinning.
3. **Local draft transcription** (L08): offline ASR producing *draft* cues bound to the same source-anchor contract — optional, unexecuted, clearly labeled draft vs human-confirmed.

### 9.2 Alternatives (plausible, bounded)
1. **Single-file SQLite project** (Audacity AU3-style, L09): atomic transactions and one-file exchange, at the cost of opacity, undo-history loss on save, and 4 GB-class exchange friction [S16]. Chosen against for v1; fallback if manifests prove unwieldy.
2. **C++/Qt shell** over the same pinned audio core (§7.1) if Python misses performance targets; contracts unchanged.
3. **GStreamer I/O layer** (L07) if the format boundary must grow; costs seek/metadata auditability — not selected.
4. **Resampler choice:** libsamplerate is the pinned default; a no-resample policy (project rate = source rate) remains a zero-risk fallback for single-rate projects, at the cost of mixed-rate projects becoming impossible.

